      createBlock("1. The Manufacturing Reality: Minimalism Has Nowhere to Hide", "h3"),
      createBlock("In both physical industrial manufacturing and digital interface engineering, visual complexity is routinely employed as an aesthetic camouflage. It is the cheapest trick in the book:"),
      createBlock("• Camouflage in Physical Products: Heavy surface textures, faux-metallic coatings, and busy decorative seams are used to hide sink marks, thermal warpage, and loose tolerances in cheap injection-molded plastics. When an industrial designer specifies a seamless unibody aluminum enclosure with a continuous 0.1mm micro-chamfer, there is zero margin for error. Every microscopic flaw is glaringly exposed."),
      createBlock("• Camouflage in UI Design: Amateur interfaces often rely on heavy drop shadows, decorative background shapes, and novelty gradients to mask weak typographic hierarchy and inconsistent spacing. In a pure minimalist layout, every baseline misalignment and awkward kerning pair immediately shatters the composition. If you want to see how good a UI designer really is, take away their colors and shadows. Watch what happens when they only have typography and whitespace."),
      createBlock("2. Signaling Theory: Zahavi's Handicap in Product Design", "h3"),
      createBlock("In evolutionary biology, Amotz Zahavi's Handicap Principle demonstrates that high-status organisms develop energetically costly traits to signal undeniable fitness. In modern product architecture, visual restraint is an economic handicap signal."),
      createBlock("Mass-market products must scream every feature on their packaging because they operate in high-distraction retail environments without brand equity. Luxury products whisper. Subtractive design communicates that a brand has the financial, engineering, and cultural confidence to sacrifice loud feature claims in exchange for architectural purity."),
      createBlock("3. Dieter Rams and 'Weniger, aber besser'", "h3"),
      createBlock("When Dieter Rams directed design at Braun throughout the 1960s and 70s, his foundational tenet was 'Weniger, aber besser' (Less, but better). Purity of form is not the absence of effort—it is the result of relentless subtractive iteration. It takes a lot of hard work to make something look this simple."),
      createBlock("Removing non-essential ornamentation shifts the entire burden of beauty onto mathematical proportions, tactile materials, and ergonomic intuition. When an interface or physical object has only three elements, those three elements must achieve absolute harmonic perfection."),
      createBlock("4. Minimal vs. Empty: The Crucial Distinction", "h3"),
      createBlock("Many junior designers mistake minimalism for simply leaving large areas blank. The result is not luxury—it is lifeless, unconsidered emptiness:"),
      createBlock("• Cheap / Empty Minimalism: Generic flat rectangles, arbitrary whitespace without rhythmic modular scale, default system fonts, and lifeless interaction states."),
      createBlock("• Disciplined High-Value Minimalism: Strict 8pt spatial pacing, harmonically calibrated typographic scale, razor-sharp visual contrast ratios, and micro-interaction states that confirm user actions with physical spring inertia. It is quiet, but it is deeply alive.")
